Mutually-referencing structs — safe asymmetric pattern
=======================================================

When two structs need to point at each other, the safe shape
is asymmetric: one direction via `&T` (refcounted, owning),
the other via `*T` (raw, non-owning back-pointer). The
parent owns the child via ARC; the child carries an unsafe
back-pointer to the parent that's only valid while the
parent is alive.

This avoids the cycle problem. An all-`&`-both-ways pattern
would form a reference cycle that ARC can't collect (sysl's
ARC has no cycle collector); the planned `unowned`
qualifier (per CLAUDE.md TRISC TODO) is what will eventually
let users mark the second direction non-owning. Until then,
the asymmetric `&` + `*` pattern is the idiomatic way to
model parent/child relationships.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    struct RmsChild
        name: string
        parent: *RmsParent

    struct RmsParent
        label: string
        child: &RmsChild

Construct + wire back-pointer
-----------------------------

Construct a parent with a fresh child whose back-pointer
starts as a null `*RmsParent`. Wire the back-pointer to `&p`
after the parent is in place. Both directions readable.

    #test
    test_parent_owns_child_child_ptr_to_parent() -> unit
        var p = RmsParent("root", new RmsChild("c0", *RmsParent(0)))
        p.child.parent = &p
        assert(p.child.name == "c0", s"forward read failed: ${p.child.name}")
        assert((*p.child.parent).label == "root", s"back read failed: ${(*p.child.parent).label}")

Reassign the child — old child's refcount drops
-----------------------------------------------

The parent's `&` field is reassigned to a fresh `new
RmsChild(...)`. The old child's refcount drops (and is
freed, since the parent was its only owner). The new
child's back-pointer is set during construction.

    #test
    test_parent_reassigns_child() -> unit
        var p = RmsParent("root", new RmsChild("first", *RmsParent(0)))
        p.child.parent = &p
        assert(p.child.name == "first", s"first child name: ${p.child.name}")
        p.child = new RmsChild("second", &p)
        assert(p.child.name == "second", s"after reassign: ${p.child.name}")
        assert((*p.child.parent).label == "root", s"back-ptr through new child: ${(*p.child.parent).label}")

Many parent/child cycles in a loop — no allocator drift
-------------------------------------------------------

N iterations, each creating a fresh parent and child. The
parent goes out of scope at end of iteration → child refcount
drops to 0 → child frees. If any backend mis-handles the
&-field-decref-on-struct-drop, repeated allocations would
exhaust memory or corrupt the allocator and a later read
would trap.

    #test
    test_many_parent_child_cycles_asymmetric() -> unit
        var i = 0
        var ok = true
        while i < 100
            var p = RmsParent(s"p$i", new RmsChild(s"c$i", *RmsParent(0)))
            p.child.parent = &p
            if p.child.name != s"c$i" then ok = false
            i = i + 1
        assert(ok, s"all 100 iterations had matching child names")

Mutation through one direction visible via the other
----------------------------------------------------

Mutating a field through the back-pointer is visible through
the forward link too — the storage is shared. Confirms that
the parent's `&` field and the child's `*` field reach the
same underlying objects.

    #test
    test_mutation_visible_both_directions() -> unit
        var p = RmsParent("orig", new RmsChild("c", *RmsParent(0)))
        p.child.parent = &p
        // Mutate parent's label via the child's back-pointer.
        (*p.child.parent).label = "mutated"
        // Read forward — parent's label is updated.
        assert(p.label == "mutated", s"forward read after back-ptr mutation: got ${p.label}")
        // Mutate child's name via the parent.
        p.child.name = "renamed"
        // Read back via the back-pointer's child link.
        assert((*p.child.parent).child.name == "renamed", s"back-ptr then forward: got ${(*p.child.parent).child.name}")
